# MVVM / WPF conventions

- Use `CommunityToolkit.Mvvm`: `[ObservableProperty]` on a private field for
  bindable state, `[RelayCommand]` on a private method for commands. Don't
  hand-roll `INotifyPropertyChanged` or `ICommand` implementations.
- ViewModels depend only on `PickMeWhatToListen.Application` types
  (`ArtistCatalogService`, `ArtistPickResult`, `Artist`). Never reference
  `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.*` from a ViewModel or View.
- **Always fully-qualify `System.Windows.Application`** in this project
  (e.g. `System.Windows.Application.Current`, and `App : System.Windows.Application`
  in `App.xaml.cs`). The sibling `PickMeWhatToListen.Application` project
  namespace shadows the unqualified `Application` name for every type under
  `PickMeWhatToListen.Wpf`, because C# namespace-member lookup wins over
  using-directives — this is a compile error (`CS0118`), not a style nit.
- The DI composition root lives entirely in `App.xaml.cs`
  (`HostApplicationBuilder` + `services.AddInfrastructure()`). Windows and
  ViewModels are resolved from the container (constructor injection), not
  `new`'d up in code-behind.
- The repo runs with `InvariantGlobalization=false` (see
  `Directory.Build.props`) specifically because WPF's binding engine needs
  real culture data at startup (`XmlLanguage.GetSpecificCulture` crashes
  under invariant mode) — don't turn it back on.
- Keep code-behind (`*.xaml.cs`) limited to `InitializeComponent`, setting
  `DataContext`, and forwarding lifecycle events (e.g. `Loaded`) into the
  ViewModel. No business logic.
- **A custom `ControlTemplate` with a bare `ContentPresenter` (no `Border`/
  `Rectangle` around it) is not hit-test visible over its full arranged
  size** — only wherever its actual content (e.g. `TextBlock` glyphs)
  paints something. `HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"` on the presenter makes
  it *occupy* the full width, but doesn't make the empty space around
  short content clickable. If you need a full-width clickable row (see
  the artist-list row `Button` in `MainWindow.xaml`), wrap the
  `ContentPresenter` in a `Border Background="{TemplateBinding
  Background}"` — `Transparent` still hit-tests, `{x:Null}` doesn't.
- **Don't rely on `ListBoxItem.IsSelected`/the ListBox's built-in
  single-selection model for anything the ViewModel also tracks
  independently** (e.g. a "selected artist" driving a side panel) — the
  two states will desync (e.g. a row staying visually highlighted after
  the ViewModel-side selection was cleared through another affordance).
  Either drive selection entirely through `SelectedItem`/`SelectedValue`
  bindings, or — if you need custom toggle-on-reclick behavior that plain
  `SelectionChanged` can't express — strip the container's default
  template/highlight entirely (see `ArtistRowContainerStyle`) and drive
  any highlight from the ViewModel's own selection state instead (see
  `RowIsSelectedConverter`, an `IMultiValueConverter` compared via a
  `MultiBinding` inside a `DataTrigger`).
